From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 18:57:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 74/79] ci: pass --break-system-packages to every pip install
 (gcp-83v1)

gcp-gtux dropped actions/setup-python in favour of the runner image's system
Python, which fixed the unresolvable libpython3.12.so.1.0 but exposed the next
layer: run 33681470318's "Install Python test dependencies" step died with
"/usr/bin/python3: No module named pip". Ubuntu's minimal python3 ships neither
pip nor ensurepip, and /usr/lib/python3.12/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED is present, so
even once python3-pip is installed a system-wide `pip install` is refused with
"error: externally-managed-environment".

The image half (python3-pip + python3-venv in the arc-runner apt list) landed in
bastion f60d755a and is live on the published :stable tag. This is the workflow
half: the four `python3 -m pip install` call sites now pass
--break-system-packages. The runner container is ephemeral and single-purpose,
so there is no system Python installation to protect -- exactly the case the
flag exists for. A venv is the cleaner answer in principle, but it would add
create/activate ceremony to four separate call sites and buy nothing inside a
throwaway container.

Restoring actions/setup-python was explicitly rejected: it would trade this pip
failure back for the nine-test libpython failure gcp-gtux fixed. The
image-provided interpreter stays; it just needed pip alongside it.

Validation: the fix is inert until CI actually runs against the new image, so
it cannot be proven locally -- the image half's liveness was verified
independently by running a throwaway pod from the published :stable tag with
imagePullPolicy=Always (python3 -m pip -> pip 24.0). Green CI on this branch is
the confirming signal.
